WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.430
Film Courage: Do you think there’s a story
structure that professionals use?

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.620
Andrew Horng, Screenwriter: I think it’s
different for everybody.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.450
I don’t think there is one story structure
that fits everything.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:13.580
A lot of people talk about three-act structure,
I kind of dog on it a little bit because I

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.500
think it really over-simplifies everything.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.770
There are some books (that I won’t name)
that say that you need to have this happen

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.269
on this page and it’s very strict.

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:33.060
I think that just as long as you have a main
character that you for some reason really

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:39.520
care about and there is conflict present and
that conflict escalates and keeps on escalating

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:46.520
and you’re still engaged as an audience
member, that’s what the professional writers

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.290
use.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:56.980
But in terms of one structure, I don’t really
believe that there is one out there.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:02.070
Film Courage: Well I know in just watching
BACK TO THE FUTURE and doing the script analysis

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:10.140
with you, different beats didn’t seem like
they were being met exactly when some of these

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.790
story structure experts say they should but
it still works.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.670
So I was just wondering what your thoughts
are?

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.880
Andrew Horng: Yeah, whatever really helps
you as a writer.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:26.470
I mean I know BACK TO THE FUTURE it does fit
into the three-act structure, but I would

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:33.140
just warn against some people…like some
writers think it’s like screenwriting religion.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.850
And I would just say that there are plenty
of films that don’t fit three-act structure.

